          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Cordelia’s loving state toward Lear. Its immediate qualification defines that love as measured by her filial bond but does not remove its truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The focal words do not undertake a new obligation or promise future conduct. The following reference to an existing bond characterizes the degree and basis of Cordelia’s love rather than creating a commitment by her.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"This is a deliberate, propositionally framed answer, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly presents herself as unable to turn the fullness of her heart into extravagant speech.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression’s core force is adequately represented as an avowal of love. Its respectful royal address and its role as an answer in Lear’s public test do not establish a separate core mode beyond T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say how much they love him while distributing portions of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal and immediately limits it to the love owed by her filial bond, resisting her sisters’ expansive declarations.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression makes no new undertaking. Cordelia’s immediately following words invoke an existing filial bond rather than promise future performance. Lear does not accept the answer as sufficient; he asks her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene supports reading the utterance as a sincere avowal, especially through Cordelia’s asides, but it does not by itself settle every question about her continuing feelings or later conduct. The assessment is confined to the marked occurrence and does not treat the neighbouring discussion of duty as a separate promise within the target.\"}"
        }
